ternal? No, only edges between i and nodes in same community count. So: community_internal[A] -= w_i_A; community_internal[B] += w_i_B.\n        - Update node_comm[node] = best_comm\n        - Note: Also need to update the w_i values for other nodes? No, because we are moving sequentially; when we later consider other nodes, we use updated community aggregates. This is the immediate update described. That's fine.\n\n- At the end, build output communities object: same keys as input communities, mapping each node to its final community (string). The output communities object must have exactly the keys from case.communities (the node IDs). So we can create dict from nodes to final community.\n\n- Return {\"communities\": final_communities, \"moves\": moves}\n\nNeed to handle validation: case must have 'edges', 'communities', 'resolution'. Must be list, dict, number. If resolution <=0? It could be positive. No random.\n\nEdge cases: If no move, moves empty list. Ensure we don't mutate case.communities.\n\nNow implement code. Use Python standard library.\n\nLet's write solve function. Use json for returning? Actually the output must be JSON-serializable object, we return dict.\n\nNow produce the response: a JSON object with key \"implementation\" containing the Python source as a string. Escape newlines, backslashes, quotes. Will need to ensure proper indentation. Use triple quotes? In JSON string, newline becomes \\n.\n\nLet's write code:\n\n```python\ndef solve(case):\n    from collections import defaultdict\n    # validate\n    if not isinstance(case, dict):\n        raise ValueError(\"case must be dict\")\n    for key in ('edges', 'communities', 'resolution'):\n        if key not in case:\n            raise ValueError(f\"missing key {key}\")\n    edges = case['edges']\n    communities_in = case['communities']\n    resolution = case['resolution']\n    if not isinstance(edges, list):\n        raise ValueError(\"edges must be list\")\n    if not isinstance(communities_in, dict):\n        raise ValueError(\"communities must be dict\")\n    if not isinstance(resolution, (int, float)):\n        raise ValueError(\"resolution must be number\")\n    # build adjacency\n    adj = defaultdict(dict)\n    total_weight = 0.0\n    for u, v, w in edges:\n        adj[u][v] = adj[u].get(v, 0) + w\n        adj[v][u] = adj[v].get(u, 0) + w\n        total_weight += w\n    # compute strengths\n    strengths = {}\n    for node in communities_in:\n        strengths[node] = sum(adj[node].values()) if node in adj else 0.0\n    # initial community aggregates\n    comm_to_nodes = defaultdict(set)  # just for internal weight? not needed\n    node_comm = {}\n    for node, comm in communities_in.items():\n        node_comm[node] = comm\n        comm_to_nodes[comm].add(node)\n    # compute community totals\n    comm_degree = defaultdict(float)\n    comm_internal = defaultdict(float)\n    for comm, nodes in comm_to_nodes.items():\n        sum_str = sum(strengths[n] for n in nodes if n in strengths)\n        comm_degree[comm] = sum_str\n    # compute internal edge weights per community\n    for u, v, w in edges:\n        if node_comm[u] == node_comm[v]:\n            comm_internal[node_comm[u]] += w\n    # nodes in sorted order\n    nodes_sorted = sorted(communities_in.keys())\n    moves = []\n    M = total_weight  # total graph weight\n    gamma = resolution\n    # main loop\n    for node in nodes_sorted:\n        curr_comm = node_comm[node]\n        s_i = strengths[node]\n        # compute w to each community\n        w_to_comm = defaultdict(float)\n        for neighbor, weight in adj.get(node, {}).items():\n            w_to_comm[node_comm[neighbor]] += weight\n        # current community w_i_A (excluding self, but no self)\n        w_i_curr = w_to_comm.get(curr_comm, 0.0)\n        # enumerate candidate communities: all communities that appear in w_to_comm plus current? but gain for current is 0, so we only consider others.\n        best_gain = 0.0\n        bes